le-class to courtly consumption, the provision of foodstuffs, and the acquisition of antiquities and holy relics. The book investigates how men and women of different social classes went to the streets, squares, and shops to buy goods they needed and wanted in daily life--or once in a lifetime. Welch draws on wide-ranging sources to expose the fears, anxieties, and social possibilities of the Renaissance marketplace and to show the impact of these attitudes on developing urban spaces. She considers transient forms of sales such as fairs, auctions, and lotteries, as well as consumers themselves. Finally, she explores antiquities and indulgences, both of which posed dramatic challenges to contemporary notions of market value and to the concept of commodification itself.--From publisher description.","publicationYear":2005,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Shopping","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-shopping-in-the-renaissance-consumer-cultures-in-italy-1400-1600-evelyn-welch"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Consumers","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-shopping-in-the-renaissance-consumer-cultures-in-italy-1400-1600-evelyn-welch"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"History","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-shopping-in-the-renaissance-consumer-cultures-in-italy-1400-1600-evelyn-welch"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Consumers, europe","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-shopping-in-the-renaissance-consumer-cultures-in-italy-1400-1600-evelyn-welch"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Consumption (economics)","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-shopping-in-the-renaissance-consumer-cultures-in-italy-1400-1600-evelyn-welch"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Einkaufen","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-shopping-in-the-renaissanc